Subject: [PATCH] MdeModulePkg/TerminalDxe: test the whole CHAR16 before the CHAR8 cast

For the terminal types other than VT-UTF8 (PC-ANSI, VT100, VT100+,
TTY, Linux, XtermR6, VT400, SCO), TerminalConOutOutputString casts a
character that is not box drawing to CHAR8 and then tests the byte, so
a character above 0x7F passes as its low byte: U+0141 is written as
'A', U+010D as a carriage return, and U+011B as ESC when OutputEscChar
is set. Test the CHAR16 itself (TerminalIsValidAscii and
TerminalIsValidEfiCntlChar take a CHAR16), and compare it, not the
byte, with ESC; a character outside ASCII is then written as '?' with
EFI_WARN_UNKNOWN_GLYPH, as the code intends.

axl-firmware's patch against edk2-stable202611, applied after
0007-shell-ctrl-s.patch to a copy of the packages it touches.

---
diff --git a/MdeModulePkg/Universal/Console/TerminalDxe/TerminalConOut.c b/MdeModulePkg/Universal/Console/TerminalDxe/TerminalConOut.c
index 10d6695..c924d04 100644
--- a/MdeModulePkg/Universal/Console/TerminalDxe/TerminalConOut.c
+++ b/MdeModulePkg/Universal/Console/TerminalDxe/TerminalConOut.c
@@ -236,17 +236,21 @@ TerminalConOutOutputString (
 
         if (!TerminalIsValidTextGraphics (*WString, &GraphicChar, &AsciiChar)) {
           //
-          // If it's not a graphic character convert Unicode to ASCII.
+          // If it's not a graphic character convert Unicode to ASCII. The
+          // whole CHAR16 is tested, not the CHAR8 it is cast to: a character
+          // above 0x7F (U+0141, say) would otherwise pass as its low byte
+          // ('A'), or reach the terminal as a control character (U+010D as
+          // CR, U+011B as ESC).
           //
           GraphicChar = (CHAR8)*WString;
 
-          if (!(TerminalIsValidAscii (GraphicChar) || TerminalIsValidEfiCntlChar (GraphicChar))) {
+          if (!(TerminalIsValidAscii (*WString) || TerminalIsValidEfiCntlChar (*WString))) {
             //
             // when this driver use the OutputString to output control string,
             // TerminalDevice->OutputEscChar is set to let the Esc char
             // to be output to the terminal emulation software.
             //
-            if ((GraphicChar == 27) && TerminalDevice->OutputEscChar) {
+            if ((*WString == 27) && TerminalDevice->OutputEscChar) {
               GraphicChar = 27;
             } else {
               GraphicChar = '?';
